## 3. Functional Requirements

### FR-1: Session Management
- **FR-1.1** The system shall allow a user to create a new chat session via an explicit action (e.g., "New Chat" button), generating a unique session identifier.
- **FR-1.2** Each session shall maintain its own isolated message history; messages from one session shall never leak into another session's context window.
- **FR-1.3** The system shall list existing sessions (id, title, created_at) so a user can resume a prior conversation.
- **FR-1.4** The system shall auto-generate a human-readable session title from the first user message (or allow a default placeholder if generation fails).
- **FR-1.5** All messages (user and assistant) within a session shall be persisted to Postgres in the order sent/received.
- **FR-1.6** The system shall manage conversation context window size for long-running sessions — once history approaches the active provider's context limit, the system shall apply a truncation and/or summarization strategy (e.g., summarize older turns into a condensed context block, retain only the most recent N turns verbatim) so long sessions degrade gracefully rather than erroring or silently dropping context.

### FR-2: LLM Provider Configuration
- **FR-2.1** The system shall support at least two interchangeable LLM providers: a cloud provider using the **Anthropic Claude SDK** (official `anthropic` Python package — not a hand-rolled REST client) and a local provider using **Ollama** (via its Python client or local HTTP API).
- **FR-2.2** Both providers shall implement a common interface (e.g., `generate(messages, system_prompt, tools) -> response`) so skill logic never branches on provider identity or imports SDK-specific types.
- **FR-2.3** Provider instantiation shall follow a **factory pattern** — a single `get_provider(name: str) -> LLMProvider` function shall construct the correct implementation from configuration, so adding a third provider (e.g., OpenAI) later requires only a new class and one factory branch, not changes to skills or routing.
- **FR-2.4** The active provider shall be selectable via configuration (environment variable and/or per-session UI toggle).
- **FR-2.5** Switching providers shall not require code changes or restarting unrelated services.
- **FR-2.6** If the configured provider is unreachable (e.g., Ollama not running, invalid API key), the system shall return a clear, user-facing error rather than a raw stack trace or silent failure.
- **FR-2.7** Where the underlying SDK/API supports it (Claude SDK streaming, Ollama streaming), the provider interface shall expose a streaming generation method, and the chat endpoint shall support streaming responses to the frontend (e.g., via Server-Sent Events) so partial output appears incrementally rather than after full completion.

### FR-3: Knowledge Base & Retrieval
- **FR-3.1** The system shall ingest transcript content from Lenny's Podcast/Newsletter source repository into a structured, queryable store.
- **FR-3.2** Ingested content shall be chunked into retrieval-sized units, each tagged with source metadata (episode title, and timestamp/section if available).
- **FR-3.3** The system shall support similarity-based retrieval via vector search, using embeddings from a **configurable embedding model** (e.g., `nomic-embed-text` via Ollama for a fully local pipeline, or an OpenAI/Voyage embedding API for the cloud path) — the embedding model shall be swappable via configuration, mirroring the LLM provider toggle, rather than hardcoded.
- **FR-3.4** Retrieval shall run automatically for the Q&A skill and shall not require the user to specify retrieval parameters.
- **FR-3.5** *(Optional, stretch)* The system may support **hybrid retrieval**, combining vector similarity search with lexical/keyword search (e.g., BM25) and merging results (e.g., via reciprocal rank fusion) to improve recall on queries with specific terminology (names, tool names, exact phrases) that pure embedding search can miss.
- **FR-3.6** Retrieved chunk sources shall be traceable — the system shall be able to report which episode(s) and chunk(s) informed a given answer.

### FR-4: Q&A Skill (Grounded Chat)
- **FR-4.1** Given a user question, the system shall retrieve relevant transcript chunks and generate an answer using only that retrieved content as factual grounding.
- **FR-4.2** If no sufficiently relevant transcript content exists for a question, the system shall state that it cannot answer from the available transcripts rather than fabricating an answer from general knowledge.
- **FR-4.3** The Q&A skill shall preserve conversational context within a session (follow-up questions shall resolve pronouns/references against prior turns).
- **FR-4.4** Every grounded answer shall **display its source attribution** to the user (e.g., episode title and chunk reference, shown as inline citations or a "Sources" list beneath the answer) rather than only being traceable via backend logs.

### FR-5: Ship30for30 Content Generation Skill
- **FR-5.1** On user request, the system shall synthesize an answer into "Ship30for30 style" output. This is **not purely a formatting transformation** — the skill shall reproduce the style's substantive characteristics, not just its visual shape:
  - A strong, specific opening hook (a claim, tension, or story beat — not a generic topic sentence)
  - A **conversational, first-person-adjacent tone** (direct address to the reader, plain language, short sentences) rather than academic or corporate register
  - **Storytelling/narrative framing** where the source material supports it (anecdote, example, or concrete scenario before or alongside the abstract point)
  - Heavy use of bullet points and bolded phrases for skimmability
  - **Actionable insights** — each major point should imply or state something the reader can do differently, not just a description of a concept
  - A clear, explicit closing takeaway
- **FR-5.2** Generated Ship30for30 content shall target approximately 1,250 words (acceptable range: 1,100–1,400).
- **FR-5.3** The skill's style rules shall be encoded as a distinct, reusable prompt/skill definition, separate from the general Q&A prompt, so it can be invoked independently of a specific question.
- **FR-5.4** Ship30for30 output shall still be grounded in transcript content when the topic is transcript-derived (i.e., it reuses FR-3/FR-4 retrieval, then reformats).

### FR-6: Skill Architecture
- **FR-6.1** Each skill (Q&A, Ship30for30, Artifact) shall be defined as an **independent module** with its own system prompt/instructions, expected inputs, and output contract.
- **FR-6.2** Skills shall be **provider-agnostic** — a skill's prompt and logic shall be written once and executed unmodified regardless of which `LLMProvider` (Claude SDK or Ollama) is active. No skill shall contain provider-specific branching.
- **FR-6.3** Skills shall be independently invocable and testable (i.e., it shall be possible to call the Ship30for30 skill directly with a topic and provider, without going through the chat/routing layer, for evaluation or debugging).
- **FR-6.4** New skills shall be addable by implementing a common skill interface (e.g., `name`, `system_prompt`, `run(context, provider) -> output`) without modifying the routing layer's core logic beyond registering the new skill.

### FR-7: Agentic Routing
- **FR-7.1** The system shall determine, per user message, which skill (Q&A, Ship30for30, Artifact) is appropriate, without requiring the user to manually select a mode for ordinary requests.
- **FR-7.2** Routing shall be based on **both message intent and conversation context** — not the current message classified in isolation. For example: a short follow-up like "now make that a post" shall route to the Ship30for30 skill by resolving "that" against the prior turn's answer, not by keyword-matching the current message alone.
- **FR-7.3** Routing logic shall be inspectable/loggable — for a given message, the system shall record which skill was invoked, the classified intent, and which prior-context signals (if any) influenced the decision.
- **FR-7.4** A user shall be able to explicitly override routing (e.g., "write this as a Ship30for30 post") and have that instruction take precedence over automatic classification.

### FR-8: Artifact Generation
- **FR-8.1** On request, or when a skill's output is inherently document-like (e.g., a Ship30for30 essay, or generated HTML/CSS), the system shall produce a discrete **artifact** separate from the inline chat message.
- **FR-8.2** Each artifact shall be typed as either `markdown` or `html`.
- **FR-8.3** Artifacts shall be persisted (linked to the message and session that produced them) and retrievable after the fact.
- **FR-8.4** The system shall support iterative refinement of an artifact (user asks for a revision; the system updates/versions the artifact rather than only appending a new unrelated one).

### FR-9: Artifact Viewer (Frontend)
- **FR-9.1** The frontend shall display a split-pane (or equivalent) layout: chat on one side, artifact rendering on the other.
- **FR-9.2** Markdown artifacts shall be rendered as formatted, styled documents (headings, bold, bullet lists, etc.), not as raw text/code.
- **FR-9.3** HTML/CSS artifacts shall be rendered as live, visually accurate UI (sandboxed execution, e.g., iframe with `srcdoc`), not as a code block.
- **FR-9.4** The viewer shall update in place when an artifact is created or revised within the same session, without requiring a full page reload.
- **FR-9.5** All artifact rendering shall happen within the application itself — the system shall not redirect to an external browser tab or separate app to display generated content.
- **FR-9.6** *(Enhancement)* The viewer shall support toggling between **rendered preview** and **raw source view** for any artifact (e.g., a "Preview / Code" tab), so a user can inspect the underlying Markdown/HTML that produced the rendered output.

