## 4. Non-Functional Requirements

### NFR-1: Performance
- Q&A responses (cloud provider) should begin streaming or return within ~5–10 seconds under normal conditions.
- Retrieval over the ingested corpus should return top-k chunks in well under 1 second (indexed lookup, not full re-scan per query).

### NFR-2: Reliability & Error Handling
- The system shall handle, without crashing: missing/invalid API keys, an unreachable Ollama instance, and a lost database connection — each with a distinct, user-legible error message.
- Partial failures (e.g., artifact generation fails but the chat message succeeds) shall not corrupt session state.

### NFR-3: Security
- API keys and database credentials shall be read from environment variables only, never committed to the repository.
- HTML artifacts shall be rendered in a sandboxed context to prevent generated script content from accessing the parent application's DOM, cookies, or storage.

### NFR-4: Maintainability
- LLM provider logic shall be isolated behind a single interface and constructed via a **factory function**, so a new provider can be added without modifying skill or routing code (see FR-2.3).
- Skills shall be organized as independent, individually testable modules with their own prompts, each decoupled from the active LLM provider (see FR-6).
- Embedding model selection shall similarly be config-driven, not hardcoded into the ingestion or retrieval code path (see FR-3.3).

### NFR-5: Usability
- A user with no prior context should be able to start a new session and get a grounded answer within their first interaction, without reading documentation.
- Errors surfaced to the user shall be in plain language, not raw exception text.

### NFR-6: Portability
- The full stack shall be runnable locally by an evaluator using their own API keys and a local Ollama install, following README instructions, without requiring access to any private infrastructure.

